[{:db/id 17592186339818 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2+4y^2=36$, oriented counterclockwise. Interpret circulation here as the sum of the four convergent improper line integrals on the open quadrant arcs, taking the one-sided limits independently at each axis intersection. The field is undefined at $(\\pm2,0)$ and $(0,\\pm3)$; ordinary circulation on the entire closed ellipse is not defined as stated in the original source." :question/worked-solution "The field is undefined where $x=0$ or $y=0$, so the original prompt does not define an ordinary line integral over all of $C$. Under the explicit improper-integral interpretation, parameterize counterclockwise by\n\n$$r(t)=(2\\cos t,3\\sin t),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2\\pi.$$\n\nFor $t$ strictly inside each of the four quadrant intervals, both coordinates are nonzero and\n\n$$\\frac{3}{y}\\,dx=\\frac{3}{3\\sin t}(-2\\sin t)\\,dt=-2\\,dt,\\qquad -\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy=-\\frac{2}{2\\cos t}(3\\cos t)\\,dt=-3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the pulled-back integrand is exactly $-5$ on each open arc. At every excluded endpoint its one-sided limit is $-5$, so every individual improper integral converges; no cancellation between divergent integrals or principal-value convention is needed. With $a_k=k\\pi/2$,\n\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=\\sum_{k=0}^{3}\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0,\\,\\delta\\downarrow0}\\int_{a_k+\\epsilon}^{a_{k+1}-\\delta}(-5)\\,dt=4\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi}{2}\\right)=-10\\pi.$$\n\nThe unchanged displayed option c is therefore correct for this explicit interpretation. This does not assign values to $F$ at the four axis intersections or establish ordinary circulation there." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
